rder  to to maintain and  and have clean water in these  communities and the year after i went  there there was  a big drought in mozambique and then the  year after that there was a flood so  climate change seems to affect the  poorest countries the most and so i  guess this this campaign is about  highlighting the need for funds that the  developing countries need we're asking  for a third of the uk's climate budget  which in total is about 14 billion  pounds so we're asking for around 4  billion pounds of that to go to the  people in the front line the people that  you can see who are highlighting sort of  i guess  represented in these sculptures  people like like them who need the money  the most in order to to help with the um  with the the climate change and the  effects it has in their communities  i guess it kind of highlights the the  the issues the very real issues that  these people have and the fragility of  water they're created by a company  called ice box and they represent the  communities in colombia malay  and and india as well and burkina faso  so they're beautiful sculptures and it  kind of reminds me of  when i was in mozambique and watching  the women in these communities having to  travel miles in order to get clean water  and the effects that that has in  communities it's kind of a feminist  issue as well if you think about it  because these women are taking out the  communities because they have to spend  most of their time during the day  walking miles and miles and miles to get  clean water and quite often that water  isn't even clean so their children get  sick and it has a huge knock-on effect  so i think that having access to clean  water as a human rights issue  as much as anything else and so you know  this is what watery do they sort of  bring attention to the  you know the issues that these  communities face and how important it is  for people all over the world especially  in developing countries to have access  to clean water  it'