ome  um producer responsibilities  but of course there's also a lot that we  can do as consumers and just last night  i went to the second-hand  september oxfam  swishing event where everyone bought  their own clothes that they didn't want  to wear anymore and everyone swapped  them it was really fun and we talked  about the fact that actually we didn't  have to spend a lot of money to get new  items whilst recycling others and you  can do this with friends family at work  and i would really  encourage everybody to think more about  shopping online  even oxfam online is such a brilliant  website now it's really it's really easy  to use let me bring let me bring in dame  julia king here you were mentioning  they're wanting the government to  intervene more but and we're a  capitalist economy how far can the  government really be  intervening in these matters and do you  would you advocate something that's like  a fashion equivalent of a sugar tax on  this industry  well i'd i'd like to see more um work  going into  helping people to understand their  carbon footprint and the impact of  things like this we know that young  people are actually very interested in  and very worried about climate change  and actually they can be part of the  change to the kind of slow fashion that  isabella was talking about so the ipcc  and the climate change committee in the  uk  have asked for more climate education  and we have a a new um secretary of  state for education in nadim zahawi just  recently been announced and i think this  is a real challenge for him  is that we should make climate education  absolutely core to the education of  young people in the uk so that people  understand the impact of fast fashion  and so that they can think about whether  that's an appropriate thing for them to  do and actually so that the people who  work in the fast fashion industry can  put pressure on their the companies they  work in to think differently about this  to think about new business models and  different ways of providing us with with  fun and enjoyable clothes that have less  impact on the environment and it's been  good to see you know some of these um  some of these apps starting up where uh  people are being encouraged to um to  sell their second-hand clothes  and give them another life all right  really good to talk to you both tonight  such important issues isabella gornall  dame julia king thank you very much for  your time on sky news that is everything  from us for tonight joining me on the  show tomorrow to discuss all of those  big climate issues will be jen allen  lecturer in international relations at  cardiff university and mohamed ado  director of power shift africa  [Music]  you

